## Writing the end-to-end test

The outline of our atomic end-to-end test takes the following form:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/atomic-http-tests/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use to_do_kernel::api::basic_actions::delete::delete_item;
    use dal::connections::sqlx_postgres::SQLX_POSTGRES_POOL;
    use dal::to_do_items::schema::ToDoItem;
    use dal::migrations::run_migrations;
    use glue::sql_commands::WIPE_DB;
    use glue::token::HeaderToken;
    use glue::urls::get_cache_url;
    use cache_client::login;
    async fn setup() {
        sqlx::query(WIPE_DB).execute(
            &*SQLX_POSTGRES_POOL
        ).await.unwrap();
        run_migrations().await;
    }
    #[tokio::test]
    async fn test_delete_item() {
        setup().await;
        . . .
    }
}

```

Here, we can see that the setup wipes the database and then runs the migrations. We then run the `setup` function at the start of the `test_delete_item` test.

Inside our `test_delete_item` function, we define some user credentials and log in to the user session cache with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/atomic-http-tests/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
let user_id = "123".to_string();
let perm_user_id = 1;
login(
    &get_cache_url().unwrap(),
    &user_id,
    60,
    perm_user_id
).await.unwrap();

```

You can see that we are not doing any error handling in the test code. This is because we are testing the code, and if it errors, we need to know, but we do not need to handle errors to prevent the system from crashing or having unexpected behavior. In our test, it takes less code and is more direct to just unwrap the result so we can see exactly where the code breaks. We can also note that we do not have to create a user. If our user session is in the cache, then we can conclude that the user is logged in and exists. This is safe because no outside user will be able to directly access the cache when we deploy our application onto a server.

We then insert the item to be deleted into the database with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/atomic-http-tests/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
let title = "test_sqlx_postgres_delete_one".to_string();
let item = ToDoItem {
    id: 1,
    title: title.clone(),
    status: "PENDING".to_string()
};
let item = sqlx::query_as::<_, ToDoItem>("
    INSERT INTO to_do_items (title, status)
    VALUES ($1, $2)
    RETURNING *"
).bind(item.title)
.bind(item.status.to_string())
.fetch_one(&*SQLX_POSTGRES_POOL).await.unwrap();
assert_eq!(item.title, title);
assert_eq!(item.status, "PENDING");
assert_eq!(item.id, 1);

```

Our assertions check that the insertion worked. We then insert the connection with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/atomic-http-tests/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
let _ = sqlx::query("
    INSERT INTO user_connections (user_id, to_do_id)
    VALUES ($1, $2)"
).bind(perm_user_id)
.bind(item.id)
.execute(&*SQLX_POSTGRES_POOL).await.unwrap();

```

We now have everything set up for our API call. We perform the API call with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/atomic-http-tests/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
let token = HeaderToken {
    unique_id: user_id.clone()
};
let outcome = delete_item(token, title).await.unwrap();
assert_eq!(outcome.done.len(), 0);
assert_eq!(outcome.pending.len(), 0);

```

Our assertions assert that there are no items returned. This is reassuring; however, we return items that are connected to the user. If we deleted the connection but not the item, we would return zero items from the API call, even if we did not technically delete the item. To properly ensure that the deletion fully worked, we do direct database calls to the connections and items table to ensure that there are no records with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/atomic-http-tests/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
let items: Vec<ToDoItem> = sqlx::query_as::<_, ToDoItem>(
    "SELECT * FROM to_do_items;"
)
.fetch_all(&*SQLX_POSTGRES_POOL)
.await
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(items.len(), 0);
let connections: Vec<(i32, i32)> = sqlx::query_as::<_, (i32, i32)>(
    "SELECT * FROM user_connections;"
)
.fetch_all(&*SQLX_POSTGRES_POOL)
.await
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(connections.len(), 0);

```

With these direct database queries, we can ensure that our deletion worked the way we wanted it to. At this point, you can explore other tests, such as what happens if the user is not authenticated or if the item does not exist. Now that you know about the methods of testing, these tests should be trivial, and covering them here would needlessly bloat the chapter. Remember, this end-to-end atomic test is running everything. Our system is going to be running the database, cache, server, and logging database. Just trying to run this manually would be time-consuming and error-prone, and there is a chance that you would later forget how to run these tests unless they are documented. If we are documenting the steps, we might as well put these steps into our test script.

