## Writing tests

For our tests, we create a test request, pass this request to the `run_request` function, process the response, and assert checks on the values of that response. For our first test, where everything is okay, we have the following signature:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/actix_server/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
#[tokio::test]
async fn test_delete_ok() {
    std::env::set_var("JWT_SECRET", "secret");
    . . .
}

```

Here, we set the environment variable so we can encode the header token. We do not know when each test will run or the order, so to be safe, we define the environment variable at the start of every test that requires the header token.

Inside our test, we create a request and make a call to the service with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/actix_server/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
let req = TestRequest::delete()
    .insert_header(ContentType::json())
    .insert_header((
        "token",
        HeaderToken{
            unique_id: "test_id".to_string()
        }.encode().unwrap()
    ))
    .uri("/delete/coding")
    .to_request();
let resp = run_request(req).await;

```

We then process the HTTP response with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/actix_server/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
let status = resp.status().as_u16();
let raw_body = resp.into_body().try_into_bytes().unwrap();
let body_str = std::str::from_utf8(&raw_body).unwrap();
let body: AllToDOItems = serde_json::from_str(
    body_str
).unwrap();

```

Finally, we assert that the response was okay and that the body contains the items that we are expecting from the mock with the following code:

```
// nanoservices/to_do/networking/actix_server/
// src/api/basic_actions/delete.rs
assert_eq!(status, 200);
assert_eq!(
    body,
    AllToDOItems::from_vec(generate_get_all_return()
).unwrap());

```

With this, our test will work just by running the test.

To test an invalid token, we must pass in an invalid token that would not decode properly. At this point, it would be a good chance to try and code the test yourself. If you attempted the invalid token test, hopefully, it looks like the following code:

